window.docContents[9].resolve({"/Elementary-Functions/___4___40-Integrals/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___40-Integrals--___4___40-Integrals":{"contents":"The real formulas below use derivative identities for indefinite integrals and\nexplicit endpoint truncations for improper integrals. Each source formula has\nits own link and preserves the source domain.\n\nThe derivative of sinh is cosh.\n\nThe derivative of cosh is sinh.\n\nThe logarithmic primitive of cosh is valid on the real line.\n\nThe logarithm of tanh(t/2) has derivative 1/sinh x on x > 0.\n\nThe Gudermannian derivative is sech x.\n\nThe logarithmic primitive of sinh is stated for x > 0.\n\nFor a nonzero real a, the integral over zero to infinity is the limit over the\ntruncated endpoints 1/R and R\nand equals pi divided by 2 tanh(pi times a divided by 2), minus 1/a.\n\nFor -pi < a < pi, endpoint truncation at 1/R and R converges to tan(a/2)/2.\n\nFor -1 < a < 1, the symmetric limit from -R to R equals the continuous\nextension of 4 pi a divided by sin(pi a), with value 4 at a=0.\n\nFor positive a,b, endpoint truncation of (tanh(ax)-tanh(bx))/x converges to log(a/b).\n\nThe arsinh antiderivative is valid for every real x.\n\nThe arcosh primitive uses the real branch for x > 1.\n\nThe artanh primitive uses -1 < x < 1.\n\nThe arcsch primitive is given on x > 0.\n\nThe arsech primitive uses 0 < x < 1.\n\nThe arcoth primitive uses the real branch for x > 1.\n\nComplex continuation in DLMF 4.40(ii) and 4.40(iv) requires continuous\nlogarithm and inverse-hyperbolic branches along paths avoiding singularities;\nthe declarations above are honest real specializations. Formula E5 is the\nGudermannian identity cross-referenced by DLMF 4.23.39--4.23.40. Copson\n(1935, p. 155) is cited for the definite integrals. The compendia paragraph\nnames Apelblat, Bierens de Haan, Gröbner--Hofreiter, Gradshteyn--Ryzhik, and\nPrudnikov as sources for further integral tables.\n\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Elementary Functions\u0009§4.40 Integrals","header":"§4.40 Integrals","id":"/Elementary-Functions/___4___40-Integrals/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___40-Integrals--___4___40-Integrals"},"/Elementary-Functions/___4___41-Sums/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___41-Sums--___4___41-Sums":{"contents":"DLMF §4.41 is a reference page for sums of hyperbolic functions. It contains\nno numbered formula displays. For worked sums and related identities, the\nsource directs the reader to the hyperbolic-function collections of\nGradshteyn and Ryzhik (2015), Chapter 1,\nHansen (1975), §43,\nPrudnikov et al. (1986a), §5.3, and\nZucker (1979).\n\nThe neighboring DLMF pages are §4.40 Integrals\nand §4.42 Solution of Triangles. No additional\nmathematical display or Lean declaration is introduced for §4.41 because the\nofficial page supplies no concrete formula record.\n\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Elementary Functions\u0009§4.41 Sums","header":"§4.41 Sums","id":"/Elementary-Functions/___4___41-Sums/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___41-Sums--___4___41-Sums"},"/Improper-integrals/3___1-Improper-integrals-with-finitely-many-exceptional-points/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Improper-integrals--3___1-Improper-integrals-with-finitely-many-exceptional-points":{"contents":"HasImproperIntegralAtTopExcept f k S I means that all points of S lie strictly to the right of\nk, and that the integral of f from k to +∞ is I. Each interval cut out by the ordered\npoints of S must converge independently at both ends. Thus the definition expresses an ordinary\nimproper integral, not a Cauchy principal value.\n\nThe recursive definitions below spell out those independent limits. The empty list is the final\ninterval ending at +∞; a nonempty list handles one singular point and then the remaining tail.\n\n\n\nFinite-dimensional formulas use the same object-first discipline. A\nMultidimensionalDomain n fixes the coordinate type Fin n → ℝ and its carrier;\ndomainIntegral is the restricted volume integral, while domainIntegrable\nrecords convergence separately. This lets Chapter 5 name a simplex, cube, or\northant without duplicating measure-theoretic plumbing.\n\n\n\nWhen the improper integral exists, its value is unique.\n\n\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Improper integrals","header":"3.1 Improper integrals with finitely many exceptional points","id":"/Improper-integrals/3___1-Improper-integrals-with-finitely-many-exceptional-points/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Improper-integrals--3___1-Improper-integrals-with-finitely-many-exceptional-points"}});